=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
A thorough end-to-end investigation found no fault anywhere in the topology.
Every layer was tested and found healthy:

**Application layer** — Apache 2.4 on web_server_1 (10.200.0.4) started cleanly
at 07:17:57 and has been running without interruption since lab boot. The error
log contains zero crash entries, and the access log confirms HTTP 200 responses
were served to host_1_1_1_1 (10.1.1.2) for every request made.

**DNS** — The dns_server's BIND zone file correctly maps web1 → 10.200.0.4, and
nslookup from host_1_1_1_1 resolves web1.local to that address without error.

**Network connectivity** — Ping from host_1_1_1_1 to 10.200.0.4 shows 0% packet
loss. A curl to http://web1.local completes in under 4 ms with an HTTP 200 and
the expected page body ("Welcome to Web Server 1"). host_2_1_1_1 also returns
200, ruling out any host-specific isolation.

**Routing (OSPF)** — All OSPF neighbors across the three core routers,
switch_dist_1_1, switch_dist_2_1, and switch_server_access are in Full state.
Routing tables on every hop contain the correct 10.200.0.0/24 prefix. The
multi-area design (area 1 for host subnets, area 0 for the backbone/server
subnet) is correctly configured with router_core_1 and router_core_2 acting as
ABRs — no route leaking or filtering anomaly.

**Firewall** — No iptables rules exist on web_server_1 or any examined device.

Root cause: There is no fault. The helpdesk ticket is a false premise — the
web portal web1.local is fully operational.

Corrective action: No remediation is needed. Advise the reporting users to
recheck their browser/endpoint configuration (cached DNS, local /etc/hosts, or
a proxy) rather than rebooting web_server_1.
=== END ===
```